window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.1","title":"Basic Probability","goal":"Compare the exact probability found by counting outcomes with the relative frequency from a seeded simulation, using the lesson's coins and two dice.","question":"A teacher wants to show her class that probability is just careful counting. She asks: if I flip two coins, what is the chance of exactly one head, and if I roll two dice, what is the chance the total is 7? Can we find the exact answer by listing every outcome, and then check it by letting the computer roll the dice many times?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"List the sample space of two coins","text":"itertools.product writes out every sequence of two flips, just like the lesson's table. Counting the sequences with exactly one head gives the exact probability, with no guessing.","code":"from itertools import product\n\nflips = list(product('HT', repeat=2))\none_head = [f for f in flips if f.count('H') == 1]\n\nprint('sample space:', flips)\nprint('exactly one head:', one_head)\nprint(f'P(exactly one head) = {len(one_head)}/{len(flips)} = {len(one_head) / len(flips):.4f}')","explain":["Load product from itertools, a standard tool that lists every combination of choices, so we do not have to write them by hand.","Write out every sequence of two flips (HH, HT, TH, TT). This list is the sample space, the complete set of outcomes.","Keep only the sequences with exactly one H. These are the outcomes that count as our event.","Print the full sample space so you can see all four equally likely outcomes with your own eyes.","Print the favourable outcomes so you can check that HT and TH are the two we expected.","Divide the favourable count by the total count: probability is favourable over possible, when outcomes are equally likely."],"meaning":"There are 4 equally likely sequences and 2 of them have exactly one head (HT and TH), so P = 2/4 = 0.5. Notice that the order matters when we list outcomes: HT and TH are different sequences, and that is why the answer is 1/2 and not 1/3.","out":"sample space: [('H', 'H'), ('H', 'T'), ('T', 'H'), ('T', 'T')]\nexactly one head: [('H', 'T'), ('T', 'H')]\nP(exactly one head) = 2/4 = 0.5000","figs":[]},{"title":"Count the ways to roll a 7","text":"Two dice give 6 x 6 = 36 equally likely pairs. We count the pairs that add to 7 and divide by 36. This exact value is the target the simulation should approach.","code":"pairs = list(product(range(1, 7), repeat=2))\nsevens = [p for p in pairs if sum(p) == 7]\nexact = len(sevens) / len(pairs)\n\nprint('pairs:', len(pairs))\nprint('pairs that sum to 7:', len(sevens))\nprint(f'exact P(sum = 7) = {exact:.4f}')","explain":["Make all 36 (die 1, die 2) pairs. Listing them is how we make sure no outcome is forgotten.","Keep the pairs whose two numbers add to 7. These are the outcomes we want.","Divide the number of winning pairs by the number of all pairs. Store it as exact, because we will compare with it later.","Print how many pairs there are, to confirm we really have 6 x 6 = 36.","Print how many of them sum to 7, the top of our fraction.","Print the exact probability, rounded to four decimals so it is easy to read."],"meaning":"Of the 36 equally likely pairs, 6 add up to 7, so the exact probability is 6/36 = 0.1667, about 1 roll in 6. A 7 is the most common total because it has more ways to happen than any other sum. This exact value is the target for the simulation next.","out":"pairs: 36\npairs that sum to 7: 6\nexact P(sum = 7) = 0.1667","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate rolling two dice","text":"A seeded generator rolls two dice again and again, so the result is the same every time you run it. The relative frequency is (times a 7 came up) divided by (rolls). Watch it settle near the exact value.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n\nfor n in [10, 100, 10_000, 1_000_000]:\n    total = rng.integers(1, 7, size=n) + rng.integers(1, 7, size=n)\n    freq = (total == 7).mean()\n    print(f'{n:>9,} rolls: relative frequency = {freq:.4f}')\n\nprint(f'exact value              = {exact:.4f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library that gives us fast random numbers.","Create a random number generator with seed 42. Fixing the seed means everyone gets the same rolls and the results can be compared.","Loop over four sample sizes, from a handful of rolls to a million, to watch what happens as we roll more.","Roll two dice n times (integers 1 to 6 from each) and add them, one total per roll.","Count the share of totals that equal 7. True counts as 1, so the mean of the True/False list is the relative frequency.","Print each frequency next to its number of rolls, with thousands separators so big numbers stay readable.","Print the exact value once more so the simulated lines can be compared with it."],"meaning":"With 10 rolls the frequency was 0.0000 (no 7 showed up at all), with 100 rolls 0.1700, with 10,000 rolls 0.1663 and with a million rolls 0.1669. The exact value is 0.1667. Few rolls give wild answers; many rolls settle close to the exact probability.","out":"       10 rolls: relative frequency = 0.0000\n      100 rolls: relative frequency = 0.1700\n   10,000 rolls: relative frequency = 0.1663\n1,000,000 rolls: relative frequency = 0.1669\nexact value              = 0.1667","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"pairs <- outer(1:6, 1:6, \"+\")      # all 36 sums\nmean(pairs == 7)                  # exact P(sum = 7)\n\nflips <- expand.grid(c(\"H\", \"T\"), c(\"H\", \"T\"))\nheads <- (flips$Var1 == \"H\") + (flips$Var2 == \"H\")\nmean(heads == 1)                  # exact P(exactly one head)\n\nset.seed(42)\nn <- 10000\nmean(sample(6, n, TRUE) + sample(6, n, TRUE) == 7)","explain":["Build a 6 by 6 table of all possible dice sums with outer(), which adds every die 1 value to every die 2 value.","The share of the 36 sums equal to 7 is the exact probability, because a True/False test averages to a proportion.","List all coin pairs with expand.grid(), R's way of crossing every choice with every other.","Count the heads in each pair by adding two True/False tests together.","The share of pairs with exactly 1 head is the exact probability for the coins.","Fix the seed so the random numbers are repeatable.","Choose how many rolls to simulate.","Roll two dice 10,000 times, add them, and take the share of 7s."],"expect":"0.1667 (6/36); 0.5; the simulated value is close to 0.1667 (for example 0.16 to 0.17)","read":"Look at the three numbers printed in order: 0.1667 for a sum of 7, 0.5 for exactly one head, and a simulated value near 0.16 to 0.17. The first two match the counting in Python exactly. The third is random, so it is close to 0.1667 but not identical.","note":"The exact numbers match Python. R's random generator differs from NumPy's, so the simulated frequency is close to 0.1667 but not identical."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (after the data exist); Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives","syntax":"INPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP #a = 1 TO 6.\nLOOP #b = 1 TO 6.\nCOMPUTE die1 = #a.\nCOMPUTE die2 = #b.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nCOMPUTE seven = (die1 + die2 = 7).\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=seven.","explain":["Start a program that builds the data itself instead of reading it from a file.","Loop over the first die, from 1 to 6.","Loop over the second die inside it, so every pair is visited.","Store the first die's value in a variable called die1.","Store the second die's value in die2.","Write out one row of data for this pair.","Close the inner loop.","Close the outer loop.","Tell SPSS there are no more rows.","End the data-building program. Now we have 36 rows.","Make a variable that is 1 when the two dice add to 7 and 0 otherwise.","Ask for the mean of that 0/1 variable, which is the probability."],"read":"In the Descriptive Statistics table, look at N (36 rows) and the Mean of seven. The mean of a 0/1 variable is the proportion of ones, so .17 (really 0.1667) is the probability of a total of 7.","note":"The loops write all 36 pairs as 36 rows. A true/false test gives 1 or 0, so the mean of seven is the probability, 0.17 (0.1667). SPSS has no one-click simulation menu, so this shows the exact count only."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 36\ngen die1 = ceil(_n/6)\ngen die2 = mod(_n - 1, 6) + 1\ngen seven = (die1 + die2 == 7)\nsummarize seven\n\nclear\nset seed 42\nset obs 10000\ngen total = ceil(6*runiform()) + ceil(6*runiform())\ngen seven = (total == 7)\nsummarize seven","explain":["Remove any data in memory so we start clean.","Make an empty data set with 36 rows, one for each pair of dice.","Create die 1: the row number divided by 6, rounded up, gives 1 six times, then 2 six times, and so on.","Create die 2: the remainder pattern repeats 1 to 6 for every value of die 1.","Make a 0/1 variable that is 1 when the dice add to 7.","Summarize it. The mean of a 0/1 variable is a probability.","Clear the data again before the simulation.","Fix the seed so the random draws are repeatable.","Make 10,000 empty rows, one per simulated roll.","Add two random dice: runiform() gives a number between 0 and 1, and ceil(6*u) turns it into 1 to 6.","Mark the rolls that total 7.","Summarize the simulation, whose mean should be near 0.1667."],"read":"In the first summarize output the Mean is .1666667, the exact probability, with Obs = 36. In the second the Mean is the simulated share of 7s from 10,000 rolls. It should be close to .1667 but will not match Python's value exactly.","note":"The first summarize gives the exact mean .1666667. The second is a simulation: close to 0.1667 but not identical to Python's, because the generators differ."},"conclusion":"Exactly one head from two coins has probability 0.5, and a total of 7 from two dice has probability 6/36 = 0.1667. The simulation agreed once we rolled many times (0.1669 for a million rolls). The caution: this counting works only because every outcome is equally likely, and a small simulation can miss the exact value by a lot.","practice":{"scenario":"A board game uses two four-sided dice (faces 1 to 4). A player needs a total of exactly 5 to move into the castle. Before playing, the designer wants to know how likely that is, and to confirm the answer with a computer simulation.","data":"Each die has faces [1, 2, 3, 4]. Use range(1, 5) to list them. For the simulation use the seed 7 and 20, 1000 and 100000 rolls.","tasks":["List all pairs of faces and count how many are possible.","How many of them total 5, and what is the exact probability?","Simulate 20, 1,000 and 100,000 double rolls with seed 7. Which frequency is closest to the exact value, and why?"],"hint":"Use product(range(1, 5), repeat=2) for the pairs, then divide the winning pairs by all pairs. In the simulation, integers(1, 5) gives faces 1 to 4.","solution":{"code":"from itertools import product\nimport numpy as np\n\npairs = list(product(range(1, 5), repeat=2))\nhits = [p for p in pairs if sum(p) == 5]\nexact = len(hits) / len(pairs)\nprint('pairs:', len(pairs), ' sum 5:', len(hits), f' exact = {exact:.4f}')\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(7)\nfor n in [20, 1000, 100_000]:\n    total = rng.integers(1, 5, size=n) + rng.integers(1, 5, size=n)\n    print(f'{n:>7,} rolls: {(total == 5).mean():.4f}')","answer":"There are 16 pairs and 4 of them total 5 ((1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1)), so the exact probability is 4/16 = 0.25. The simulated frequencies are 0.2500 for 20 rolls, 0.2420 for 1,000 and 0.2477 for 100,000. The big run is closest in general because more rolls average out the luck; the 20-roll value matched only by chance.","out":"pairs: 16  sum 5: 4  exact = 0.2500\n     20 rolls: 0.2500\n  1,000 rolls: 0.2420\n100,000 rolls: 0.2477","figs":[]}}});
